   "transcript": "Presenter: As well as holding water, soil also acts as storage for carbon, especially when it’s frozen, and with climate change, that might be the best place for it. But with global warming, the permafrost and the tundra defrostand the results may be disastrous. Dr Ian Hartley of Exeter University explains.\nDr Ian Hartley: Permafrost contains an incredible amount of organic matter, an incredible amount of carbon. We didn’t realise just how much carbon was actually present there, so more extensive surveys, especially in North America, have revealed that perhaps there’s three times as much carbon there as we’d previously thought and this means there is more carbon purely locked away in these frozen permafrost soils than there is in all the trees in the world and the atmosphere put together.\nThat carbon has been present in these soils for thousands of years, locked away since the soil began to freeze. When the systems have actually been looked at and have been measured, we can actually see tundra ecosystems that are no longer absorbing carbon but are actually releasing it and by radiocarbon dating, the CO₂ that’s being released from these systems, we’re able to demonstrate that the reason why they are releasing carbon today is because that old frozen carbon that had been locked away when the permafrost was present is now starting to decompose as the permafrost thaws.\nSo trying to understand not just how much carbon is present there, how much organic matter is present at these higher latitudes, but also how it might respond to climate change in the future, is a massive question. The amounts of carbon that could be released would be equivalent perhaps to the whole of the current rates of deforestation taking place in the tropics.\nPresenter: Ian Hartley from Exeter University. Richard, that does sound serious. The world gets warmer, the permafrost melts and that releases megatons more carbon that has been trapped for thousands of years. Is this a runaway effect?\nRichard: Yeah, I mean, this is one of the points I mentioned earlier about why soils have become very high on the political and scientific agenda, because I guess the concern, as Ian pointed out, is that as soils warm, particularly in these northern latitudes, they store vast amounts of carbon. That carbon, as the soils warm, the microbes get more active and it’s pushing that carbon dioxide back into the atmosphere, which is basically like accelerating the rate of climate change.\nAnd it’s not just in these high Arctic systems that Ian referred to. It’s also very close to home in peatlands. For example, in northern England and in Scotland, there are similar concerns about warming and drying of those peats leading to increased breakdown of that organic matter and it’s supplied back to the atmosphere.\nPresenter: And you said earlier that soil is the third largest carbon pool. To what extent can something so enormous be managed?\nRichard: I think the places where we can manage is more in lowland agricultural systems, in grasslands, for example, where there is the possibility of changing the way that we manage the land. This is an area of research that we’re focusing on at the moment in Manchester with colleagues in Aberdeen University and also of the Centre for Ecology and Hydrology, and what we’re doing is looking at how different plant species, and in particular the characteristics of their root systems, impact on the soil.\nSo what we’re effectively trying to do is look for the best combinations of grass species and identify the characteristics of the roots of those species which best help to build carbon in soil. So essentially, what we’re looking at is really trying to build diverse mixtures of grassland species with particular root characteristics which are beneficial to the sequestration of carbon in soil."
